  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday Night through Monday)\nIssued at 1117 PM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nA mid/upper level synoptic trough will amplify along the West \nCoast Tuesday into Wednesday. As the trough deepens and nudges \ninland, the summer ridge and its axis will shift eastward, \nbecoming positioned over the northern Gulf coast by Thursday.\nLonger range models are suggesting the ridge will eventually\nretrograde back over the Southern Plains late in the weekend and\ninto early next week. Unfortunately, the result of all this will \nnot really change the overall local weather pattern...it will \nstill be consistently hot and mostly dry through the remainder of \nthe workweek, with daily afternoon high temperatures reaching the \nupper 90s and lower 100s and overnight temperatures generally in \nthe upper 70s and lower 80s. Heat headlines will likely be needed \nfor at least a portion of the forecast area each afternoon for the\nforeseeable future. Please continue to take precautions to stay \ncool and hydrated when outdoors this week.\n\nAs the upper-level ridge becomes stationed over the southeast\nCONUS late this week, latest suite of global models and ensembles\nshow a weak frontal boundary pushing into and stalling across \ncentral KS Thursday night. Consensus in the models keep the \nfrontal boundary well north of the area through the weekend. This \nis in contrast to previous model runs, where they showed the front\nstalling closer to the KS/OK border through the upcoming weekend,\nproducing low shower and thunderstorm chances for parts of far \nnortheast OK and far northwest AR. With models now stalling the \nfront farther north, rain chances seem unlikely through the \nforecast period at this time. \n\nMejia",
